        <article class="content-block">
          <h2>Contact</h2>
          <p>GitHub Issues are the primary support route because they make reproducible bugs easier to track. For questions, feature ideas, or general discussion, use GitHub Discussions instead.</p>
          <p><a class="button primary" href="https://github.com/eslutz/A-Playa-Named-Gus/issues/new/choose">Open a GitHub Issue</a> <a class="button" href="https://github.com/eslutz/A-Playa-Named-Gus/discussions">GitHub Discussions</a></p>
          <p>Email is also available at <a href="mailto:support@ericslutz.dev?subject=GUS%20SUPPORT">support@ericslutz.dev</a>. Keep the subject prefix <strong>GUS SUPPORT</strong> so support mail is routed correctly.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="content-block">
          <h2>Before reporting a bug</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>Confirm the Jellyfin server URL works in Safari on the same network.</li>
            <li>Note the platform: iPhone, iPad, Apple TV, Apple Vision Pro, or Mac.</li>
            <li>Include the app version, Jellyfin server version, and whether the server uses HTTP or HTTPS.</li>
            <li>Describe the media type and playback path, but do not include private server URLs, passwords, tokens, or personal library screenshots.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="content-block">
          <h2>FAQ</h2>
          <div class="faq">
            <h3>What do I need to use Gus?</h3>
            <p>You need access to a Jellyfin server. Gus is a client app and does not include hosted media or a hosted Jellyfin account.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq">
            <h3>Does Gus support Quick Connect?</h3>
            <p>Quick Connect support is part of the sign-in flow when the connected Jellyfin server supports it.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq">
            <h3>Why can local server discovery ask for network permission?</h3>
            <p>Find Local Servers uses the local network to discover Jellyfin servers. Manual URL entry remains available if you do not want to use discovery.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq">
            <h3>Can I use an HTTP server?</h3>
            <p>Self-hosted Jellyfin servers often use HTTP on trusted local networks. For remote access, use HTTPS and server configuration appropriate for your network.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq">
            <h3>Why does playback fail for some files?</h3>
            <p>Playback depends on the media, Jellyfin server capabilities, network conditions, and AVKit support on the current Apple platform. Files your device's hardware can decode play directly at original quality; everything else is transcoded by your server. If a file fails, include format details and whether it direct-plays or transcodes on the server.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq">
            <h3>Can I limit streaming quality?</h3>
            <p>Yes. Settings &rsaquo; Playback &rsaquo; Streaming Quality caps the playback bitrate (Maximum, High, Medium, or Low). Maximum lets compatible files play at original quality; lower settings ask your server to transcode for constrained networks.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq">
            <h3>How do I turn off episode auto-play?</h3>
            <p>Episodes auto-play the next episode when one finishes. Turn this off with Settings &rsaquo; Playback &rsaquo; Auto-Play Next Episode.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq">
            <h3>Where are downloads available?</h3>
            <p>Downloads are available on iPhone, iPad, Mac, and Apple Vision Pro where supported by the app and server. Apple TV streams only.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq">
            <h3>What is Gus Cinema?</h3>
            <p>Gus Cinema is the Apple Vision Pro immersive playback path for supported scenarios. Availability depends on platform support, media format, and the current release state.</p>
          </div>
        </article>
